717:
For the purposes of this article, excess noise shall include:
(1) Sound emitted from a motor vehicle that is audible ten feet from the vehicle;
(2) Sound emitted from a building or dwelling that is audible at the property line of the building or dwelling;
(3) Sound emitted from other sources that is audible 100 feat from its source; and
(4) Any sound that registers 85 decibels or more at a distance of 100 feet from its source on a sound level meter or other instrument designed to measure sound level.

718:
The standards that may be considered in determining whether a violation of the provisions of this article exists may include, but not be limited to, the following:
(1) The level of the noise;
(2) The nature of the noise and whether it is usual or unusual;
(3) Whether the origin of the noise is natural or unnatural;
(4) The level and intensity of the background noise, if any;
(5) The proximity of the noise to residential sleeping facilities, hospitals, churches, facilities for the care of the elderly, ill or mentally ill, as well as any other area which historically is treated by the community as a quiet area;
(6) The density of the inhabitation of the area within which the noise emanates;
(7) The nature and zoning of the area within which the noise emanates;
(8) The time of day or night the noise occurs;
(9) Whether the noise is recurrent, intermittent, or constant.

775:
(a) It shall be unlawful for any person to:
(1) Negligently and without authority to injure or destroy any meter, pipe, conduit, wire, line, post, lamp or other apparatus belonging to the city or a company engaged in the manufacture or sale of electricity, gas, water, sewer, telephone or other public services;
(2) Negligently and without authority to prevent a meter from properly registering the quantity of such service supplied;
(3) In any way to interfere with the proper action of the city or such company;
(4) Negligently to divert any services of the city or such company; or
(5) Otherwise negligently and without authority to use or cause to be used, without the consent of the city or such company any service manufactured, sold or distributed by the city or such company.
(b) Where there is no evidence to the contrary, the person performing any of the illegal acts set forth in subsection (a) of this section and the person who with knowledge of such violation receives the benefit of such service without proper charge as a result of the improper action shall be presumed to be responsible for the act of tampering or diversion.
(c) It shall be unlawful for any person intentionally and without authority to injure or destroy any traffic-control equipment, sign, vehicle, recreation equipment, recreation property, lock, padlock, building or other such property belonging to the city.
(Code 1992, § 11-3; Ord. No. 87-3, 7-7-1987)
State Law reference— Damaging, injuring or interfering with property of public utility companies, municipalities or political subdivisions, O.C.G.A. § 16-7-25.

786:
It is an exception to, and shall be an affirmative defense to any charge of violations of the terms of this article, if the minor is involved in any of the following activities:
(1) Emergency errand. The minor is upon an emergency errand directed by his parent, guardian, or other adult person 21 years of age or older having the lawful care and custody of the minor;
(2) School activity. The minor is returning directly home from a school activity;
(3) Employment. The minor is traveling to, or returning directly home from, lawful employment that makes it necessary to be in the places referenced in section 36-135 during the proscribed period of time;
(4) Constitutional rights. The minor is attending or traveling directly to or from an activity involving the exercise of First Amendment rights of free speech, freedom of assembly, or free exercise of religion;
(5) Travel. The minor is engaged in interstate and intrastate vehicular travel through the city with parental consent;
(6) Residence. The minor is on the sidewalk adjoining the property where the minor resides or on the property where the minor resides or on either side of or across the street from the property where the minor resides and the adult or the owner or resident of that property has given permission for the minor to be there; and
(7) Marriage. The minor is married or has been married pursuant to state law.

