856:
(a) All precious metals and gems dealers shall keep books, in permanent form, wherein shall be entered, in English and in legible form, an accurate, detailed description of all precious metals and gems sold to them. Such description shall include:
(1) The name of the maker of the article;
(2) Any identifying mark or number and a description of the kind of material of which it is made so that such article can be reasonably and definitely identified;
(3) A photograph of the item, made at the time and place of purchase, (which photograph shall be attached to the page whereon the corresponding entry is made);
(4) The name, address and age of the person by whom such article was deposited or sold as verified by a valid, government-issued picture identification of the seller;
(5) The date and hour the transaction was made;
(6) A photograph or copy of the identification of the seller, made at the time and place of purchase, (which photograph shall be attached to the page whereon the corresponding entry is made); and
(7) The signature of the seller.
(b) The permanent record book shall be maintained for each purchase of precious metals and/or gems for a period of three years from the date of purchase of the last entry in said record book. Entries shall appear chronologically and shall be signed, in ink, by the purchaser at the end of each complete entry. No blank lines may be left between the entries. No obliterations, alterations or erasures may be made. Corrections shall be made by drawing a line of ink through the entry without destroying its legibility. Such book and the articles pledged or sold shall at all times be subject to inspection and examination by the police. Upon refusal of the proprietors at any time to submit their shops to a thorough inspection by the police, the shops shall be immediately closed and the permit and license revoked.
(Ord. No. 2014-3, 8-5-2014)
